  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nCurrently, a short wave trough is situated over the Upper Midwest \nand is moving eastward over the Great Lakes region. At the surface, \nthe corresponding low pressure sits over western Quebec with a \nfrontal boundary draped from Minnesota into northern Kansas. Broad \nridging extends over the western U.S. and into the Southern Plains \nplacing the forecast area under northwesterly flow. As we progress \nthrough the morning hours, the frontal boundary will sweep across \nthe state turning winds out of the north, and eventually the \nnortheast. \n\nThis cold front won't bring much in terms of cooler temperatures, \nbut rather a reinforcing shot of dry air. High temperatures today \nwill reach into the middle 70s for central Kansas with highs close \nto 80s degrees near the KS/OK border. For Monday, highs in the \nmiddle 70s are expected areawide. The drier air is prominent on \nwater vapor imagery and anomalously low PWATs expected through \nMonday. Mostly clear skies and zero precip chances will continue \nthrough Monday night. \n\nRidging will begin to start building into the region on Tuesday, \nwith the ridge center remaining over the Desert Southwest and the \nSouthern Plains. A weak perturbation in the pattern on Tuesday night \nassociated with a trough digging into the Great Lakes may slow the \nwarming trend. But increasing heights will lead to afternoon \ntemperatures reaching into the middle 80s by the end of the week. A \nfew clouds associated with passing waves are expected for Tuesday \ninto Wednesday, as well as on Saturday. Otherwise, dry conditions \nwill persist through the end of the forecast period.",
